In 9-dot text modes the VGA repeats a character's 8th column in its 9th only for C0h-DFh, with the attribute
controller's line graphics bit: the box drawing characters join up. tiny386 (as QEMU's VGA) did it for B0h-DFh too,
so the shades B0h-B2h got a doubled dot in every cell: a text-mode program's shaded background (FreeDOS EDIT's
desktop) showed vertical stripes. The shades get the background colour there, as on the real card.

--- a/vga.c
+++ b/vga.c
@@ -913,7 +913,7 @@
                                     fgcol, bgcol);
                 } else {
                     dup9 = 0;
-                    if (ch >= 0xb0 && ch <= 0xdf && (s->ar[0x10] & 0x04))
+                    if (ch >= 0xc0 && ch <= 0xdf && (s->ar[0x10] & 0x04))
                         dup9 = 1;
                     vga_draw_glyph9(dst, stride, font_ptr, cheight,
                                     fgcol, bgcol, dup9);
